\section{Introduction}

Earthquake rupture emerges from nonlinear interactions between elastic strain,
viscoelastic relaxation, pore‑pressure diffusion, and external tidal forcing.
Traditional numerical models exhibit secular energy drift, irreversibility, and
loss of phase‑space fidelity. AST‑TSDME addresses these limitations by embedding
tectonic evolution within a Hamiltonian, symplectic, time‑reversible manifold,
enabling precise stress inversion and active mitigation.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Governing Hamiltonian
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Hamiltonian Framework}

Let $\mathcal{M}$ denote the lithosphere‑asthenosphere phase space with
coordinates $(q,p)$ representing crustal displacement and momentum/strain‑rate.
The total Hamiltonian is:
\begin{equation}
H(q,p,t) = T(p) + V_{\text{elastic}}(q) + V_{\text{viscoelastic}}(q)
+ V_{\text{poroelastic}}(q) + V_{\text{tide}}(q,t).
\end{equation}

The evolution is governed by Hamilton's equations:
\begin{equation}
\dot{q} = \nabla_p H, \qquad \dot{p} = -\nabla_q H.
\end{equation}

